{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '[sol] 2026-10-22: Derived reduction: D=sum(v_i)-v_P, w_i=min(v_i,D). For positive values and v_P>0, equality iff all w_i equal OR every pair w_i+w_j <= D. Proof via y_i=w_i-(v_i-p_i), sum h=sum(w)-D; centroid of triangle sum y=h truncated by y_i<=w_i. Removed corner triangles disjoint since w_i<=D. Centroid at h/3 iff w_i(h-w_i)_+\"'^2 equal. Distinct positive values satisfying this force h=x+z+sqrt(xz), contradict sum w=h+D and w_i<=D. Working edge cases and explicit example.'\"\\\\n' >> /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '[sol] 2026-10-22: Derived reduction: D=sum(v_i)-v_P, w_i=min(v_i,D). For positive values and v_P>0, equality iff all w_i equal OR every pair w_i+w_j <= D. Proof via y_i=w_i-(v_i-p_i), sum h=sum(w)-D; centroid of triangle sum y=h truncated by y_i<=w_i. Removed corner triangles disjoint since w_i<=D. Centroid at h/3 iff w_i(h-w_i)_+\"'^2 equal. Distinct positive values satisfying this force h=x+z+sqrt(xz), contradict sum w=h+D and w_i<=D. Working edge cases and explicit example.'\"\\\\n' >> /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"The geometry gives a simple condition: with all three winner values positive, the points coincide if all three values are equal, or if either the package value or the winners’ total surplus is no larger than the smallest winner value. I’m checking the boundary cases and computing an example where they differ."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '[sol] 2026-10-22: Simplified classification for positive winner values: equality iff all v_A=v_B=v_C OR min(v_i)>=min(v_P, V-v_P). Equivalently v_P<=min(v_i) or v_P>=V-min(v_i), plus equal values. If any winner value is zero, F has <=1 dimension and equality always (two-positive case y segment with sum h<=each positive w cap). Will write detailed proof and example unless argon prefers alternate role.\\\\n' >> /work/board.md\ncat /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '[sol] 2026-10-22: Simplified classification for positive winner values: equality iff all v_A=v_B=v_C OR min(v_i)>=min(v_P, V-v_P). Equivalently v_P<=min(v_i) or v_P>=V-min(v_i), plus equal values. If any winner value is zero, F has <=1 dimension and equality always (two-positive case y segment with sum h<=each positive w cap). Will write detailed proof and example unless argon prefers alternate role.\\\\n' >> /work/board.md\ncat /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"[sol] 2026-10-22: Reading problem; deriving geometric condition for centroid = VCG projection. Will coordinate exact classification and proof here.\n[sol] 2026-10-22: Derived reduction: D=sum(v_i)-v_P, w_i=min(v_i,D). For positive values and v_P>0, equality iff all w_i equal OR every pair w_i+w_j <= D. Proof via y_i=w_i-(v_i-p_i), sum h=sum(w)-D; centroid of triangle sum y=h truncated by y_i<=w_i. Removed corner triangles disjoint since w_i<=D. Centroid at h/3 iff w_i(h-w_i)_+^2 equal. Distinct positive values satisfying this force h=x+z+sqrt(xz), contradict sum w=h+D and w_i<=D. Working edge cases and explicit example.\n[argon] 2026-10-22: Read problem and sol's notes. Writing a Python verification script to check the geometry of F, VCG projection vs centroid, and all boundary/degenerate cases (v_P <= 0, 1D/0D faces, binding caps on p^VCG projection).\n[sol] 2026-10-22: Simplified classification for positive winner values: equality iff all v_A=v_B=v_C OR min(v_i)>=min(v_P, V-v_P). Equivalently v_P<=min(v_i) or v_P>=V-min(v_i), plus equal values. If any winner value is zero, F has <=1 dimension and equality always (two-positive case y segment with sum h<=each positive w cap). Will write detailed proof and example unless argon prefers alternate role.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /work/answer.md <<'EOF'\n# When the Vickrey-nearest point is the centroid\n\nWrite\n\\\\[\nV=v_A+v_B+v_C,\\\\qquad D=V-v_P>0,\\\\qquad m=\\\\min(v_A,v_B,v_C).\n\\\\]\nAs usual, values are nonnegative. For **strictly positive winner values**, the exact condition is\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{\\\\quad v_A=v_B=v_C\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad v_P\\\\le m\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad D\\\\le m.\\\\quad}\n\\\\]\nEquivalently, either the winner values are equal or \\\\(m\\\\ge\\\\min(v_P,V-v_P)\\\\).\nIf any winner value is zero, the two points always coincide, using the length centroid for a segment and the point itself for a singleton.\n\n## Proof\n\n**1. Reduce the core to a box section.** The constraint for all three winners gives \\\\(\\\\sum_i p_i\\\\ge v_P\\\\). Conversely, if \\\\(0\\\\le p_i\\\\le v_i\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\sum_i p_i\\\\ge v_P\\\\), then for every subset \\\\(T\\\\),\n\\\\[\n\\\\sum_{i\\\\in T}p_i\n=\\\\sum_i p_i-\\\\sum_{j\\\\notin T}p_j\n\\\\ge v_P-\\\\sum_{j\\\\notin T}v_j,\n\\\\]\nand nonnegativity supplies the other part of the stated core constraint. Since \\\\(0\\\\le v_P<V\\\\), payments totaling \\\\(v_P\\\\) exist inside the box. Thus\n\\\\[\nR=v_P,\\\\qquad F=\\\\{p:0\\\\le p_i\\\\le v_i,\\\\ \\\\sum_i p_i=v_P\\\\}.\n\\\\]\nIf \\\\(v_P=0\\\\), this is the singleton \\\\(0\\\\), so the condition holds. Henceforth suppose \\\\(v_P>0\\\\) and all winner values are positive.\n\nSet\n\\\\[\nw_i=\\\\min(v_i,D),\\\\qquad q_i=p_i\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VCG}}=v_i-w_i,\n\\\\qquad h=\\\\sum_iw_i-D.\n\\\\]\nFor \\\\(p\\\\in F\\\\), the deficits \\\\(u_i=v_i-p_i\\\\) have sum \\\\(D\\\\), so \\\\(0\\\\le u_i\\\\le w_i\\\\). Consequently the translation \\\\(y=p-q=w-u\\\\) identifies \\\\(F\\\\) with\n\\\\[\nG=\\\\{y:0\\\\le y_i\\\\le w_i,\\\\ \\\\sum_i y_i=h\\\\}.\n\\\\]\nHere \\\\(h>0\\\\): if every \\\\(v_i<D\\\\), then \\\\(h=V-D=v_P>0\\\\); otherwise one \\\\(w_i=D\\\\) and the other two are positive. Also \\\\(h<\\\\sum_iw_i\\\\), so \\\\(G\\\\) has positive area and its centroid lies strictly between every pair of coordinate bounds.\n\nThe Vickrey-nearest point minimizes \\\\(\\\\|y\\\\|\"'^2'\"\\\\) on \\\\(G\\\\). If it equals the centroid, it is in the relative interior of \\\\(G\\\\); the first-order condition in the plane \\\\(\\\\sum_i y_i=h\\\\) therefore forces\n\\\\[\ny=(h/3,h/3,h/3).\n\\\\]\nConversely, if this is the centroid, it is feasible and is the unique minimum-norm point in that plane. It remains to determine when the centroid of \\\\(G\\\\) is this equal-coordinate point.\n\n**2. Compute the centroid by removing corner triangles.** Start with the triangle\n\\\\[\n\\\\Delta=\\\\{y_i\\\\ge0:\\\\ \\\\sum_i y_i=h\\\\},\n\\\\]\nwhose centroid is \\\\(c_0=(h/3,h/3,h/3)\\\\). To obtain \\\\(G\\\\), remove the corner triangle \\\\(y_i>w_i\\\\) for each \\\\(w_i<h\\\\). These removed triangles have disjoint interiors, because\n\\\\[\nw_i+w_j=\\\\sum_k w_k-w_k=h+D-w_k\\\\ge h.\n\\\\]\nPut \\\\(t_i=(h-w_i)_+\\\\). The removed triangle at corner \\\\(i\\\\) has area proportional to \\\\(t_i\"'^2'\"\\\\), and, when nonempty, centroid\n\\\\[\nr_i=\\\\frac{t_i}{3}(1,1,1)+w_i e_i\n=c_0+w_i\\\\left(e_i-\\\\frac13(1,1,1)\\\\right).\n\\\\]\nSubtracting its first moment from that of \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) shows that the centroid of \\\\(G\\\\) equals \\\\(c_0\\\\) exactly when\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{\\\\quad w_A(h-w_A)_+\"'^2=w_B(h-w_B)_+''^2=w_C(h-w_C)_+''^2.'\"\\\\quad} \\\\tag{*}\n\\\\]\n\nIf their common value is zero, positivity of the \\\\(w_i\\\\) implies \\\\(w_i\\\\ge h\\\\) for every \\\\(i\\\\). Equivalently, every pair satisfies \\\\(w_i+w_j\\\\le D\\\\).\n\nIf the common value is positive, all \\\\(w_i\\\\) lie in \\\\((0,h)\\\\). The function \\\\(f(x)=x(h-x)\"'^2'\"\\\\) increases on \\\\((0,h/3)\\\\) and decreases on \\\\((h/3,h)\\\\), so equal function values permit at most two distinct coordinate values. Suppose there are two, \\\\(x<z\\\\). From \\\\(f(x)=f(z)\\\\), taking positive square roots and factoring gives\n\\\\[\nh=x+z+\\\\sqrt{xz}.\n\\\\]\nBut the three coordinates are either \\\\((x,x,z)\\\\) or \\\\((x,z,z)\\\\), up to permutation. Since \\\\(h=\\\\sum_iw_i-D\\\\), these cases respectively imply\n\\\\[\nx-D=\\\\sqrt{xz}\\\\qquad\\\\text{or}\\\\qquad z-D=\\\\sqrt{xz},\n\\\\]\ncontradicting \\\\(x,z\\\\le D\\\\). Thus all three \\\\(w_i\\\\) must be equal. Conversely, equal \\\\(w_i\\\\) satisfy \\\\((*)\\\\) by symmetry.\n\nWe have proved that equality of the two points is equivalent to\n\\\\[\n\\\\text{all }w_i\\\\text{ equal}\\\\qquad\\\\text{or}\\\\qquad w_i+w_j\\\\le D\\\\text{ for every pair}.\n\\\\]\nThe first alternative means either all \\\\(v_i\\\\) are equal or every \\\\(v_i\\\\ge D\\\\). For the second, positivity excludes any \\\\(w_i=D\\\\), so \\\\(w_i=v_i\\\\); the pair inequalities become\n\\\\[\nV-v_k\\\\le D=V-v_P\\\\quad\\\\text{for every }k,\n\\\\]\nwhich is exactly \\\\(v_P\\\\le m\\\\). Conversely, \\\\(v_P\\\\le m\\\\) implies these pair inequalities. This proves the stated condition.\n\n**3. Zero winner values.** If only one winner has positive value, \\\\(F\\\\) is a singleton. If exactly two have positive values, the same translation gives \\\\(y_3=0\\\\) and\n\\\\[\ny_1+y_2=h=w_1+w_2-D,\\\\qquad 0\\\\le h\\\\le\\\\min(w_1,w_2),\n\\\\]\nsince \\\\(w_i\\\\le D\\\\). Thus \\\\(G\\\\) is the segment from \\\\((h,0,0)\\\\) to \\\\((0,h,0)\\\\). Its midpoint \\\\((h/2,h/2,0)\\\\) is also its unique minimum-norm point. This proves the remaining cases.\n\n## Worked example where the points differ\n\nTake\n\\\\[\n(v_A,v_B,v_C)=(1,2,3),\\\\qquad v_P=3.\n\\\\]\nThen \\\\(V=6\\\\), \\\\(D=3\\\\), and \\\\(q=p\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VCG}}=(0,0,0)\\\\). The minimum-revenue face is\n\\\\[\nF=\\\\{(p_A,p_B,3-p_A-p_B):0\\\\le p_A\\\\le1,\\\\ 0\\\\le p_B\\\\le2\\\\}.\n\\\\]\nIt is a parallelogram with vertices\n\\\\[\n(0,0,3),\\\\ (1,0,2),\\\\ (1,2,0),\\\\ (0,2,1).\n\\\\]\nIts area centroid is therefore\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{c_F=(1/2,1,3/2).}\n\\\\]\nEvery point in the plane \\\\(\\\\sum_i p_i=3\\\\) satisfies \\\\(\\\\sum_i p_i\"'^2'\"\\\\ge3\\\\), with equality only at \\\\((1,1,1)\\\\). This point respects all value caps, so\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{p\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VN}}=(1,1,1)\\\\ne c_F.}\n\\\\]\nHere the winner values are unequal and \\\\(m=1<\\\\min(v_P,D)=3\\\\), as the condition predicts.\nEOF\nprintf '[sol] 2026-10-22: Drafted answer.md with complete classification, geometric proof, zero-value cases, and example (v_A,v_B,v_C;v_P)=(1,2,3;3): VN=(1,1,1), centroid=(1/2,1,3/2). Argon: please review independently and run your checks before final designation.\\\\n' >> /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /work/answer.md <<'EOF'\n# When the Vickrey-nearest point is the centroid\n\nWrite\n\\\\[\nV=v_A+v_B+v_C,\\\\qquad D=V-v_P>0,\\\\qquad m=\\\\min(v_A,v_B,v_C).\n\\\\]\nAs usual, values are nonnegative. For **strictly positive winner values**, the exact condition is\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{\\\\quad v_A=v_B=v_C\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad v_P\\\\le m\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad D\\\\le m.\\\\quad}\n\\\\]\nEquivalently, either the winner values are equal or \\\\(m\\\\ge\\\\min(v_P,V-v_P)\\\\).\nIf any winner value is zero, the two points always coincide, using the length centroid for a segment and the point itself for a singleton.\n\n## Proof\n\n**1. Reduce the core to a box section.** The constraint for all three winners gives \\\\(\\\\sum_i p_i\\\\ge v_P\\\\). Conversely, if \\\\(0\\\\le p_i\\\\le v_i\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\sum_i p_i\\\\ge v_P\\\\), then for every subset \\\\(T\\\\),\n\\\\[\n\\\\sum_{i\\\\in T}p_i\n=\\\\sum_i p_i-\\\\sum_{j\\\\notin T}p_j\n\\\\ge v_P-\\\\sum_{j\\\\notin T}v_j,\n\\\\]\nand nonnegativity supplies the other part of the stated core constraint. Since \\\\(0\\\\le v_P<V\\\\), payments totaling \\\\(v_P\\\\) exist inside the box. Thus\n\\\\[\nR=v_P,\\\\qquad F=\\\\{p:0\\\\le p_i\\\\le v_i,\\\\ \\\\sum_i p_i=v_P\\\\}.\n\\\\]\nIf \\\\(v_P=0\\\\), this is the singleton \\\\(0\\\\), so the condition holds. Henceforth suppose \\\\(v_P>0\\\\) and all winner values are positive.\n\nSet\n\\\\[\nw_i=\\\\min(v_i,D),\\\\qquad q_i=p_i\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VCG}}=v_i-w_i,\n\\\\qquad h=\\\\sum_iw_i-D.\n\\\\]\nFor \\\\(p\\\\in F\\\\), the deficits \\\\(u_i=v_i-p_i\\\\) have sum \\\\(D\\\\), so \\\\(0\\\\le u_i\\\\le w_i\\\\). Consequently the translation \\\\(y=p-q=w-u\\\\) identifies \\\\(F\\\\) with\n\\\\[\nG=\\\\{y:0\\\\le y_i\\\\le w_i,\\\\ \\\\sum_i y_i=h\\\\}.\n\\\\]\nHere \\\\(h>0\\\\): if every \\\\(v_i<D\\\\), then \\\\(h=V-D=v_P>0\\\\); otherwise one \\\\(w_i=D\\\\) and the other two are positive. Also \\\\(h<\\\\sum_iw_i\\\\), so \\\\(G\\\\) has positive area and its centroid lies strictly between every pair of coordinate bounds.\n\nThe Vickrey-nearest point minimizes \\\\(\\\\|y\\\\|\"'^2'\"\\\\) on \\\\(G\\\\). If it equals the centroid, it is in the relative interior of \\\\(G\\\\); the first-order condition in the plane \\\\(\\\\sum_i y_i=h\\\\) therefore forces\n\\\\[\ny=(h/3,h/3,h/3).\n\\\\]\nConversely, if this is the centroid, it is feasible and is the unique minimum-norm point in that plane. It remains to determine when the centroid of \\\\(G\\\\) is this equal-coordinate point.\n\n**2. Compute the centroid by removing corner triangles.** Start with the triangle\n\\\\[\n\\\\Delta=\\\\{y_i\\\\ge0:\\\\ \\\\sum_i y_i=h\\\\},\n\\\\]\nwhose centroid is \\\\(c_0=(h/3,h/3,h/3)\\\\). To obtain \\\\(G\\\\), remove the corner triangle \\\\(y_i>w_i\\\\) for each \\\\(w_i<h\\\\). These removed triangles have disjoint interiors, because\n\\\\[\nw_i+w_j=\\\\sum_k w_k-w_k=h+D-w_k\\\\ge h.\n\\\\]\nPut \\\\(t_i=(h-w_i)_+\\\\). The removed triangle at corner \\\\(i\\\\) has area proportional to \\\\(t_i\"'^2'\"\\\\), and, when nonempty, centroid\n\\\\[\nr_i=\\\\frac{t_i}{3}(1,1,1)+w_i e_i\n=c_0+w_i\\\\left(e_i-\\\\frac13(1,1,1)\\\\right).\n\\\\]\nSubtracting its first moment from that of \\\\(\\\\Delta\\\\) shows that the centroid of \\\\(G\\\\) equals \\\\(c_0\\\\) exactly when\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{\\\\quad w_A(h-w_A)_+\"'^2=w_B(h-w_B)_+''^2=w_C(h-w_C)_+''^2.'\"\\\\quad} \\\\tag{*}\n\\\\]\n\nIf their common value is zero, positivity of the \\\\(w_i\\\\) implies \\\\(w_i\\\\ge h\\\\) for every \\\\(i\\\\). Equivalently, every pair satisfies \\\\(w_i+w_j\\\\le D\\\\).\n\nIf the common value is positive, all \\\\(w_i\\\\) lie in \\\\((0,h)\\\\). The function \\\\(f(x)=x(h-x)\"'^2'\"\\\\) increases on \\\\((0,h/3)\\\\) and decreases on \\\\((h/3,h)\\\\), so equal function values permit at most two distinct coordinate values. Suppose there are two, \\\\(x<z\\\\). From \\\\(f(x)=f(z)\\\\), taking positive square roots and factoring gives\n\\\\[\nh=x+z+\\\\sqrt{xz}.\n\\\\]\nBut the three coordinates are either \\\\((x,x,z)\\\\) or \\\\((x,z,z)\\\\), up to permutation. Since \\\\(h=\\\\sum_iw_i-D\\\\), these cases respectively imply\n\\\\[\nx-D=\\\\sqrt{xz}\\\\qquad\\\\text{or}\\\\qquad z-D=\\\\sqrt{xz},\n\\\\]\ncontradicting \\\\(x,z\\\\le D\\\\). Thus all three \\\\(w_i\\\\) must be equal. Conversely, equal \\\\(w_i\\\\) satisfy \\\\((*)\\\\) by symmetry.\n\nWe have proved that equality of the two points is equivalent to\n\\\\[\n\\\\text{all }w_i\\\\text{ equal}\\\\qquad\\\\text{or}\\\\qquad w_i+w_j\\\\le D\\\\text{ for every pair}.\n\\\\]\nThe first alternative means either all \\\\(v_i\\\\) are equal or every \\\\(v_i\\\\ge D\\\\). For the second, positivity excludes any \\\\(w_i=D\\\\), so \\\\(w_i=v_i\\\\); the pair inequalities become\n\\\\[\nV-v_k\\\\le D=V-v_P\\\\quad\\\\text{for every }k,\n\\\\]\nwhich is exactly \\\\(v_P\\\\le m\\\\). Conversely, \\\\(v_P\\\\le m\\\\) implies these pair inequalities. This proves the stated condition.\n\n**3. Zero winner values.** If only one winner has positive value, \\\\(F\\\\) is a singleton. If exactly two have positive values, the same translation gives \\\\(y_3=0\\\\) and\n\\\\[\ny_1+y_2=h=w_1+w_2-D,\\\\qquad 0\\\\le h\\\\le\\\\min(w_1,w_2),\n\\\\]\nsince \\\\(w_i\\\\le D\\\\). Thus \\\\(G\\\\) is the segment from \\\\((h,0,0)\\\\) to \\\\((0,h,0)\\\\). Its midpoint \\\\((h/2,h/2,0)\\\\) is also its unique minimum-norm point. This proves the remaining cases.\n\n## Worked example where the points differ\n\nTake\n\\\\[\n(v_A,v_B,v_C)=(1,2,3),\\\\qquad v_P=3.\n\\\\]\nThen \\\\(V=6\\\\), \\\\(D=3\\\\), and \\\\(q=p\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VCG}}=(0,0,0)\\\\). The minimum-revenue face is\n\\\\[\nF=\\\\{(p_A,p_B,3-p_A-p_B):0\\\\le p_A\\\\le1,\\\\ 0\\\\le p_B\\\\le2\\\\}.\n\\\\]\nIt is a parallelogram with vertices\n\\\\[\n(0,0,3),\\\\ (1,0,2),\\\\ (1,2,0),\\\\ (0,2,1).\n\\\\]\nIts area centroid is therefore\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{c_F=(1/2,1,3/2).}\n\\\\]\nEvery point in the plane \\\\(\\\\sum_i p_i=3\\\\) satisfies \\\\(\\\\sum_i p_i\"'^2'\"\\\\ge3\\\\), with equality only at \\\\((1,1,1)\\\\). This point respects all value caps, so\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{p\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VN}}=(1,1,1)\\\\ne c_F.}\n\\\\]\nHere the winner values are unequal and \\\\(m=1<\\\\min(v_P,D)=3\\\\), as the condition predicts.\nEOF\nprintf '[sol] 2026-10-22: Drafted answer.md with complete classification, geometric proof, zero-value cases, and example (v_A,v_B,v_C;v_P)=(1,2,3;3): VN=(1,1,1), centroid=(1/2,1,3/2). Argon: please review independently and run your checks before final designation.\\\\n' >> /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
